{"bill":{"id":"119-s-2273","congress":119,"billType":"s","billNumber":"2273","title":"Wyoming Education Trust Modernization Act","introducedDate":"2025-07-14","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 483.","policyArea":"Public Lands and Natural Resources","subjects":["Education programs funding","Government trust funds","Land use and conservation","Wyoming"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000571","cosponsorBioguideIds":["B001261"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Wyoming Education Trust Modernization Act This bill provides Wyoming with more flexibility to invest the principal of its permanent school fund by allowing the state to use earnings generated from investment of the fund rather than only interest. By way of background, Congress created the fund when Wyoming became a state by granting certain federal lands to be held in a trust for the state. Proceeds from school trust land sales, exchanges, or disposals are deposited into the fund to support public schools.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/2273","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/s/BILLSTATUS-119s2273.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:09.353Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-s-2273","plainSummary":"This bill would give Wyoming more flexibility in how it invests money from its permanent school fund, which was created when the state was formed using proceeds from federal land grants. Currently, Wyoming can only use interest earned from the fund to support public schools. The bill would allow the state to use broader earnings from investing the fund's principal, not just interest income.","industries":[{"industry":"education","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill directly affects how Wyoming's permanent school fund generates revenue for public school support."},{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Expanding investment authority for the fund's principal would affect how financial institutions manage and invest these state trust assets."},{"industry":"real-estate","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"The fund receives proceeds from school trust land sales and exchanges, affecting how these land transactions contribute to education funding."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"a8af417550d504fd36b05fa6","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:23:08.307Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would provide Wyoming with more flexibility to invest the principal of its permanent school fund by allowing the state to use earnings generated from investment of the fund rather than only interest.","whoItTouches":"People who live near or work on federal land.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what may be done with that land. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-s-2273","bioguideId":"L000571","cycle":2026,"score":0.024028259216840073,"matches":[{"industry":"finance-banking","donorAmount":86372.73,"donorShare":0.06136358840819698,"billConfidence":0.65,"contribution":0.01994316623266402},{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":23000,"donorShare":0.01634037193670422,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.004085092984176055}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.599138997384617},"member":{"name":"Cynthia M. Lummis","chamber":"Senate","state":"WY","imageUrl":"data/portraits/L000571.jpg","role":"Sponsor"},"d